#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083075 :question/id #uuid "b2f8d797-75e8-5ac8-82e7-1c5a9788e6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6252" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2\\cdot 0-1=-1\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523091 :question-generator/id #uuid "dea88c52-5d24-5fd3-bddd-4f56915dffb2" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186083280.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0735403-c4cc-5f1c-b3f3-f20e814b4e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439583} {:answer/id #uuid "18c5b8fc-dc2a-5f66-aba9-2fb2139b0e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18c5b8fc-dc2a-5f66-aba9-2fb2139b0e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439584} :answer-field/id #uuid "45f84d85-7a93-543f-94bb-a475c80511cd" :answe