,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=2{e}^{0}=2\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38170285-5909-5a87-b81e-7614b331e561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254153} {:answer/id #uuid "61b95ad1-c677-5a1d-9693-7fe23f0898f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254545} {:answer/id #uuid "74ee3251-ef46-57f3-8565-b37dbcb4bbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254706} {:answer/id #uuid "9f808726-09d3-5692-a435-5070f152cddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255075} {:answer/id #uuid "cd387a61-32a4-5106-b17f-5c138d6c2ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd387a61-32a4-5106-b17f-5c138d6c2ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255494} :answer-field/id #uuid "7461ea8a-3c02-5867-96b2-21f3b9f03a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256136}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e5d7b42-493e-5694-92f6-e6ea1c2f15fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122946" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7(t-1),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the points at which the normal to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6({t}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d597a98-c055-5f99-9bee-b68fbbdd90cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253785} {:answer/id #uuid "0f02ed52-8673-5cc6-908c-e93ec89237c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253801} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc3b08-7caf-5e27-b46d-574424299686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254482} {:answer/id #uuid "80913870-a077-54f8-a853-f63a26091309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254807} {:answer/id #uuid "e09500e3-b088-5b78-9a17-f963b264a9fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80913870-a077-54f8-a853-f63a26091309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254807} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5601a63-8c5d-5677-8eb5-dd5f3c21d7f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256329}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "259b7265-587e-5b30-9b76-d6e0205b048e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122947" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi t)-2t,\\,y={e}^{2t},\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{t}-2=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=2{e}^{2\\times (1/2)}=2e\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dcd11c4-604f-5bca-bd31-a170bd84a811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dcd11c4-604f-5bca-bd31-a170bd84a811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317946} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec3bc6ba-dba0-565a-9d3e-6d25c11cdc38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318918}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d9076ce-d69d-5ee0-95b8-de3533c91e39" :question/math-academy-id "q-350403" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-8t,\\,y=2{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe normal to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t-8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-8 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =8 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(4) & =6{(4)}^{2}=96\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dccbdf4-6b20-5048-8475-7edb8b5f47ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dccbdf4-6b20-5048-8475-7edb8b5f47ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317614} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8a6d76-f112-5663-9241-788ae415e493" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095b8b92-1a17-5015-a26c-15163e676f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "095b8b92-1a17-5015-a26c-15163e676f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317600} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb60be2-41b3-5c75-b56a-529c11e5a236" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318774}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0473c92-6bbb-51d0-9d33-3793374dc209" :question/math-academy-id "q-350414" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+8,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-5,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $t=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb0847e-f077-52b5-a2dc-3b5be04d8d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439947} {:answer/id #uuid "4427a1db-576b-5e6d-b443-4988d488b644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4427a1db-576b-5e6d-b443-4988d488b644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439948} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8fddcc0-226d-57ff-8494-03c04378375d" :answer-field/key "$t=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440331} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c94317bf-98d7-5e75-83f2-d1c446fe74a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439949} {:answer/id #uuid "50cf1385-eb6b-5d99-a20e-9cfe7c73c596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c94317bf-98d7-5e75-83f2-d1c446fe74a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439949} :answer-field/