}, \\frac{10\\pi}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d66d1357-e0d7-58c9-a48f-bd62562836f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d66d1357-e0d7-58c9-a48f-bd62562836f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523080} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ef8185-3b01-5cc2-a040-a6ce07a57c3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "396284fb-66e1-5043-b407-7357db7c21ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "396284fb-66e1-5043-b407-7357db7c21ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523036} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e4eda86-0324-54cf-b300-9a498fb8794e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523041}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da8bb941-6503-5014-b798-77c51b79d5e5" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+11,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) & =-6\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right) & =6\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d337060-1e09-500e-a725-817b12e948cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523023} {:answer/id #uuid "4c24db76-c425-5c85-8ebb-d6b5749020ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523040} {:answer/id #uuid "a7936598-9d1f-55c4-9dd9-7abc95040343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523060} {:answer/id #uuid "bb44dca9-460c-5f8e-a9be-d5ddfc0acf26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523068} {:answer/id #uuid "c509673f-074a-50e2-8f60-18ed364b8a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c509673f-074a-50e2-8f60-18ed364b8a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523074} :answer-field/id #uuid "afcde732-d4fd-5d99-bec4-a5da89e670fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523065}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee8bc31a-d21a-5520-b1c9-d397836ee8dd" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{5t},\\,y=4{t}^{2}-8t,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5{e}^{5t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t-8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t-8=0\\,⟹\\,t=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(1)=5{e}^{5}=5 e^{5}\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2066933a-e4ff-5d1b-ba2e-6721a2c0beee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527379} {:answer/id #uuid "2078c7d8-986e-5ee7-aa63-51282c836e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527380} {:answer/id #uuid "84da7f6b-0d7b-57aa-b191-586787cecf86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527564} {:answer/id #uuid "926b354b-27ea-5d05-9a3e-0c0abaf06914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527596} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d7db2e-a3aa-5e45-b181-a6699caf9368" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "926b354b-27ea-5d05-9a3e-0c0abaf06914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527596} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bffa8b9-64ed-5f9b-b634-ff2bf41846ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527816}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a6c8612-f46a-5c05-98fb-598338ec8310" :question/math-academy-id "q-122948" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=1-{e}^{3t},\\,y=4{t}^{3},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n12{t}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=-3{e}^{3(0)}=-3\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e20b7b8-1e36-5f41-9b20-23e8c8a99d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527548} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f3cdeb-e33c-5ce4-8657-a90ffab15d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527648} {:answer/id #uuid "c86857df-a7de-55aa-bacf-e267b3b6b6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527710} {:answer/id #uuid "cc85ea11-173a-5a41-bdb2-37322253a23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527720} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a66421-49c0-5cde-af39-90c0d6317866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc85ea11-173a-5a41-bdb2-37322253a23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527720} :answer-field/id #uuid "245554b4-93be-561f-a213-763e72867446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527813}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b067330-3793-583e-b08f-f7fd948895ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-122836" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}+2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-3t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4t({t}^{2}+1)=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=3(0)^{2}-3=-3\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c0acc55-8e3c-53a4-99b4-5214b54f65a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527368} {:answer/id #uuid "5caf1410-dca6-5dc8-b2ee-176e9278f3aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527483} {:answer/id #uuid "88e0bd31-ef4f-539a-8935-8ba431e9c39a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527575} {:answer/id #uuid "eec6ec5f-a4e6-54e8-99cf-c1af592a1edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527771} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f10241-5a93-5b9a-b65e-e6638b2d8c44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eec6ec5f-a4e6-54e8-99cf-c1af592a1edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527771} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba41f178-68e0-563a-b1ab-e2a541af6814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527885}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab667ef1-091e-5d1a-aa96-b7c759cdc55d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75268" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-t},\\,y=5{t}^{2}+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-{e}^{-t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=10t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n10t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=-{e}^{-0}=-1\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186590066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60983a3b-640e-5929-9231-408eaeffb73f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589462} {:answer/id #uuid "6deafb26-8a2b-56fa-b018-a1064b792c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589489} {:answer/id #uuid "a6eb7e37-e62f-56fc-aaf6-2dfa78349065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589607} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c7c37a-f49d-58e3-9ee2-364d62709dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589738} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dab76e-3ea7-5f1d-8494-a4ced94a7c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6eb7e37-e62f-56fc-aaf6-2dfa78349065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589607} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff6d8f8b-f38e-585c-afa9-53a4052ca6f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186589970}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e494bcd2-c935-5682-9b4b-f7adb27c6c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-75270" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5{e}^{3t},\\,y=6{t}^{2}-9,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=15{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n12t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=15{e}^{3\\cdot 0}=15\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53fac786-edb1-50f4-b7c0-4981772d6f59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53fac786-edb1-50f4-b7c0-4981772d6f59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724962} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bc960f4-7d98-58f1-b946-a18f0fc8d04e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724958} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895ad559-f08f-5928-97ba-4ccb54f53349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "895ad559-f08f-5928-97ba-4ccb54f53349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724966} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6851b0-151c-595c-8688-823918b39e36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724970}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d6192ee-d81b-576f-9c34-2cc6df4d5f6c" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t^2+3}{t-1},\\qquad y=t^2+1,\\qquad -2<t<4,\\quad t\\ne1.\n$$\n\nDetermine all values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers in **ascending order**.\n\nThe normal is horizontal at $t=$ {{field-1}} and $t=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal when the tangent is vertical. We therefore need $x'(t)=0$ and $y'(t)\\ne0$ at parameters in the given domain.\n\nUsing the quotient rule for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=\\frac{2t(t-1)-(t^2+3)}{(t-1)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{t^2-2t-3}{(t-1)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{(t-3)(t+1)}{(t-1)^2},\\\\\ny'(t)&=2t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $t\\ne1$, the denominator of $x'(t)$ is nonzero. Hence $x'(t)=0$ exactly when\n\n$$\n(t-3)(t+1)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=-1\\ \\text{or}\\ t=3.\n$$\n\nBoth values lie in $-2<t<4$ and neither equals the excluded parameter $1$. The value $t=1$ is not a candidate: the original curve is undefined there.\n\nCheck the other derivative:\n\n$$\ny'(-1)=-2\\ne0,\\qquad y'(3)=6\\ne0.\n$$\n\nThus the normal is horizontal at $t=-1$ and $t=3$, in that order. Although $y'(0)=0$, we have $x'(0)=-3\\ne0$, so $t=0$ gives a horizontal tangent and a vertical normal instead."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "211d97f3-d99d-58c2-8fd5-7b6fd4b3e35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724957} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfec1b5-6cfa-57ef-ac45-2751184da881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724963} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcff6a0-d5b5-538d-a8d2-70089ddf0fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724965} {:answer/id #uuid "9a52cf59-ee06-539c-a1f5-732093fa9e54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724968} {:answer/id #uuid "c4bb8da7-dae2-53e8-b803-83cb53948be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a52cf59-ee06-539c-a1f5-732093fa9e54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724968} :answer-field/id #uuid "31ad0f3a-3bb6-5f20-9659-e6f10d2e32de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724959}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7309ec21-e629-5015-953e-195cabe13f77" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t^2-6t,\\qquad y=t^3+t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal when the tangent is vertical. We therefore need $x'(t)=0$ and $y'(t)\\ne0$.\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2t-6,\\qquad y'(t)=3t^2+1.\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t-6=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=3.\n$$\n\nAt this parameter,\n\n$$\ny'(3)=3(3)^2+1=28\\ne0.\n$$\n\nThus the tangent is vertical and the normal is horizontal at $t=3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f67c4a5-db13-5858-8707-67f41c1afede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f67c4a5-db13-5858-8707-67f41c1afede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724960} :answer-field/id #uuid "953bb1a2-99c6-5a30-a4c3-2778c7445a26" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724967} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "406f776a-dbcd-5466-9bfb-6c12dec0604a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "406f776a-dbcd-5466-9bfb-6c12dec0604a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724961} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd4d186d-2e39-5a2e-968e-45d3746a4252" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724972}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ebce9dd-f1fe-5297-b3dc-3c91b5f33ad0" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+2,\\\\\ny&=2\\sin\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)-1,\\\\\n&\\hspace{1em}0\\le t<2\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDetermine all values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in **ascending order**.\n\nThe normal is vertical at $t=$ {{field-1}} and $t=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical when the tangent is horizontal. We therefore need $y'(t)=0$ and $x'(t)\\ne0$.\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=-3\\sin\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right),\\\\\ny'(t)&=2\\cos\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $y'(t)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=0,\\\\\nt-\\frac{\\pi}{6}&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+k\\pi,\\\\\nt&=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}+k\\pi,\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly $k=0$ and $k=1$ place $t$ in $0\\le t<2\\pi$, giving $t=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$. Check the other derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)&=-3\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=-3\\ne0,\\\\\nx'\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)&=-3\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)=3\\ne0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal is vertical at $t=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$, in that order."}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"}]}
